-- Example: schedule the never-paid retention sweep.
--
-- Pure SQL, no secrets: the function lives in the database
-- (99_supabase_migration_never_paid_retention_v1.sql) and cron calls it
-- directly. For a claimed Marketplace property that has never paid and has not
-- seen a person for 180 days, it records property.data_purged in
-- product_events, then deletes the imported booking history, the import jobs,
-- open onboarding findings, unaccepted invites, the stored PMS credential and
-- the connection row. The property, its members, rules, room types, closed
-- periods and answered findings stay. Anything paying, trialing, past due or
-- that has ever paid is never touched. The migration header lists every table.
--
-- Daily is plenty: nothing is due until a property has been quiet for six
-- months. Off-peak, after the claim sweep at 09:10.
--
-- 0) Ensure the extension exists:
--    create extension if not exists pg_cron;
--
-- 1) Look before the first real run (deletes and records nothing). A dry run
--    stops at p_max_properties like a real one, so ask for all of them:
--    select public.never_paid_retention_sweep(p_dry_run => true, p_max_properties => 1000);
--
-- 2) Schedule (09:20 UTC daily):
--    select cron.schedule(
--      'never-paid-retention-sweep',
--      '20 9 * * *',
--      $$ select public.never_paid_retention_sweep(p_max_properties => 5); $$
--    );
--
-- Inspect:    select jobid, jobname, schedule, active from cron.job;
-- Runs log:   select * from cron.job_run_details where command like '%never_paid_retention_sweep%'
--               order by start_time desc limit 20;
-- Unschedule: select cron.unschedule('never-paid-retention-sweep');
--
-- The whole run is one statement, so one transaction: nothing it locks is
-- released until it commits, and a property it has locked cannot be activated
-- or reconnected until then. That is why the scheduled run takes 5 properties
-- (the default is 25), each with at most 40 deletes of 50,000 reservations;
-- whatever is left is finished the next day. What was swept:
--   select occurred_at, property_name, properties
--     from product_events where event = 'property.data_purged'
--    order by occurred_at desc limit 20;
